The camera's pictures with lettering in them (ILCE-7M2 firmware 4.01)

Many things the camera shows as "text" are pictures Sony drew in Univers: the focal lengths in the SteadyShot
list, the menu page numbers, AWB, MF, ISO, the creative style names, the drive mode numbers, ... They live in
/usr/share/app/image_*.uxc, which the UI (im.elf) maps. These scripts find the lettering in them and draw it
again in Comic Relief Bold; the font patcher (liba7hacks.so --font-patcher) then writes the new pictures over the old ones in the camera's
in-memory copy of those files, like the font.

The .uxc picture files
  "uxd\x07" header, then tables of 20-byte records: u32 id (0x1a......), u32 flags (0xe0000000 = a compressed
  picture; 0xe8000000 = something else, left alone), u16 width, u16 height, u32 length, u32 offset. The
  picture's data starts at (the record's own position + 4 + offset) and is length bytes long.
  The data is Sony's LZ77 (the one their firmware updates use, cf. the Linux kernel's lib/lz77 and fwtool's
  lz77 module): blocks of up to 4096 output bytes, each a type byte 0xf0 then groups of a flag byte (bit
  0 first: 0 = one literal byte, 1 = a match of two bytes: length code in the high nibble, lengths 3..16, 32,
  64; distance 1..4095 in the other 12 bits) and ending with a match of distance 0.
  The output (unpacked by the camera's hardware LZ77 unit, /dev/ldec) is the picture as the GPU wants it:
  RGBA4444 (grey in R, G and B), width and height rounded up to 8, in 8x8 tiles row by row, the pixels in each
  tile in Morton order, rows top down.

Scripts
  uxc.py      reads the records and unpacks pictures
  lz.py       the LZ77 decoder, and an encoder for the same format (greedy, a bit tighter than Sony's)
  tile.py     8x8 Morton tiles to rows and back
  ocr.py      finds lettering: splits a picture into light (or dark) shapes and matches each against Univers
              and two condensed sans fonts; groups them into lines of text
  confirm.py  has Tesseract read every line found, from just the shapes matched; mkpics keeps the lines where
              both agree
  render.py   erases a line (its fill, edge and shadow) and draws it in Comic Relief Bold over the same place:
              same height (caps / x-height per run of characters), natural width where there is room around
              it, squeezed (to 0.62 at most, then smaller) where there isn't; the same fill grey, dark edge and
              soft shadow as the original, measured from it
  mkpics.py   builds image_*.uxc.pics.json: per picture, only the pixels we drew (in the camera's order),
              the length and hash of the new picture packed by ../../uxclz.c (quick mode where it fits,
              else the cheapest), and a hash of the original; Sony's own pixels are not in it
  mkpicheader.py  turns those into ../../picpatch.h for a7hacks.c (the font patcher unpacks the original from the file in RAM,
              puts our pixels in, packs it and writes it only if length and hash come out as computed here)
  overrides.py  lines whose reading is settled another way, used by mkpics: a person's reading from the
              review page (answers.json; only where it fits the shapes found), or a word confirmed
              elsewhere in the pictures (only if it differs in one letter at most, never a digit), or listed in
              its KNOWN table (the menu's page numbers, which the scan misses or misreads)
  badges.py   the creative style badges (Std., Vivid, ... and their numbered versions 1-6): made from
              their shapes, a fixed set of words in a fixed layout (used by overrides.py); likewise the D-R,
              DRO and HDR boxes; OFF and AUTO beside an icon are settled there too
  answers.json  the review page's answers: per group, what Tesseract and the shapes read, and what it reads
  hue.py      the accent colour: Sony's orange turned HUE degrees (env HUE, default 75: green), as a table
              RGB444 -> RGB444 for the warm colours, applied to the pictures that use the orange accent
              (not photos, not the red ones, not the gold auto-mode icons); the camera applies the same
              table (pic_hue in picpatch.h) to pictures marked with the top bit of _raw. HUE=0: no change
  qa.py       before/after sheets for checking by eye (buildc: in colour; about half the pictures are
              coloured, the orange 'selected' ones, and render.py keeps their colours)

  cc -O2 -DUXCLZ_MAIN -x c -o uxclz ../../uxclz.c
  python3 scan.py; python3 confirm.py; python3 overrides.py; python3 mkpics.py
  python3 mkpicheader.py ../../picpatch.h PC image_cmn_43.uxc.pics.json PE image_EVF.uxc.pics.json \
      PD image_DSLR_Only_43.uxc.pics.json PS image_DSC_Only_43.uxc.pics.json
  (UXCAPP: the folder with the camera's image_*.uxc; UNIVERS: the camera's Univers as a .ttf)

Left alone: lines where the readings disagree and nobody settled them, lettering cut out of a box (the shapes
found aren't its letters), and lettering next to lettering that wasn't confirmed (half a label would be worse
than none). Light lettering on an opaque box (the creative style badges) is drawn when it has three letters or
more; the box inside gets its own colour back where the old letters were.
